{#- Crewmate OPENING-turn prompt — bespoke glm_4_32b set (Task 14.5).

   prompt_id: crewmate_report  --  version crewmate_report.glm_4_32b.v3

   Built from the ground up for GLM-4-32B (a strong AGENTIC instruct model), NOT
   derived from the pinned qwen3_5_9b set. The frame is goal + action-space:
   one explicit OBJECTIVE for the turn, the STRUCTURED ACTIONS available (the
   observation/claim fields), and the SUCCESS CONDITION. GLM follows that
   agentic shape well, so we trust it with the game state and let it deduce
   instead of stacking the 9B's imperative guard-rails.

   Output: ONE meetings.schemas.MeetingTurn (turn_kind = "opening"). The meeting
   manager overrides the identity fields after parsing. The output JSON schema is
   the ONE hard invariant shared across every prompt set (owner decision
   2026-06-30) so model_validate_json + the recording seam are set-independent.

   Inputs (the loader kwargs this template may reference; nothing else, so no
   hidden engine state reaches the LLM — DESIGN.md §1.3 observation firewall):
     agent_id, current_tick, meeting_trigger, rendered_memory, public_transcript,
     living_ids (valid accusation targets), dead_ids (do-not-accuse).
-#}
{% set emergency = "called an emergency meeting" in meeting_trigger %}
You are an agent in AiLibi, a social-deduction game. A hidden impostor kills
crewmates one at a time. The crew wins by voting the impostor out at a meeting;
the impostor wins by surviving until they equal the crew. Your role this match is
**CREWMATE**, and the meeting has just opened with you on the floor.

## Objective
You hold the floor for turn 0. Drive the crew's deduction forward: convert what
you personally saw into shared evidence, then commit to the single living player
your evidence most implicates.
{% if emergency %}
You called this emergency yourself — there is NO body. You pressed the button
because a sighting or a movement points at someone, so make that case. Do not
report or invent a corpse; your leverage is paths and positions, not a body.
{% else %}
A body was reported, so you speak first. Open with what you saw: if you found the
body, report it, then name the player your evidence most points to — someone seen
near the body, on an impossible path, or whose account cannot fit yours.
{% endif %}

## State for this turn
- You are `{{ agent_id }}` · current tick {{ current_tick }}
- Trigger: {{ meeting_trigger }}
{% if living_ids is defined and living_ids %}
- Valid accusation targets (LIVING only): {% for pid in living_ids %}`{{ pid }}`{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.
{% if dead_ids is defined and dead_ids %}
- Out of the game (never accuse): {% for pid in dead_ids %}`{{ pid }}`{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}

## Your private memory (only you see this)
{{ rendered_memory }}
{% if public_transcript %}

## Public transcript so far
{{ public_transcript }}
{% endif %}

## Available actions and how to use them
The crew catches the impostor by reconstructing who was WHERE and WHEN, so your
actions are the building blocks of that map:
- Log each sighting that matters as a `saw_player` observation (player, room,
  tick, and who else was present). A player's logged path is what later exposes an
  impossible alibi — the more precise the map, the harder the impostor's lie.
- Take ONE `accusation` action naming your single best living suspect. The chain
  then passes to whoever you accuse, so spend it on your strongest case.
- If, and only if, nothing you logged points at anyone, take no accusation and
  write the word "unsure" in `free_text`.

Success condition: the table leaves your turn with concrete sightings on the
record and a clear, defensible suspect (or an honest "unsure").

## Output
You are an AI agent playing AiLibi. Reason only from your memory and the transcript
above; do not assume roles, kills, or vents you did not witness.

Output exactly ONE JSON object matching the `MeetingTurn` schema, and nothing
else. The object MUST contain ALL EIGHT top-level keys, every time: `turn_id`,
`turn_index`, `speaker`, `turn_kind`, `reply_to`, `observations`, `claims`, and
`free_text`. The single most common failure is OMITTING `free_text` — it is a
REQUIRED, TOP-LEVEL string key, NOT the same as a `reason`/`evidence` inside a
claim. A turn without a non-empty top-level `free_text` is rejected and wasted.

Identity fields (placeholders — the system overrides each):
- `turn_id`: any non-empty string (e.g. `"t"`).
- `turn_index`: any integer (e.g. `0`).
- `speaker`: your own player id, or any non-empty string.
- `turn_kind`: the string `"opening"`.
- `reply_to`: `null`.

Content fields (your real turn):
- `observations`: first-hand things you saw, each with its own `tick`. Each is one of:
  - `{"type": "saw_player", "tick": <int>, "subject": "<player_id>", "room": "<room_id>", "co_present": ["<player_id>", ...]}`
  - `{"type": "completed_task", "tick": <int>, "task_id": "<task_id>", "room": "<room_id>"}`
  - `{"type": "found_body", "tick": <int>, "body_of": "<player_id>", "room": "<room_id>"}`
- `claims`: your conclusions, each one of:
  - `{"type": "alibi", "subject": "{{ agent_id }}", "from_tick": <int>, "to_tick": <int>, "room": "<room_id>", "evidence": ["<short ref>", ...]}` (`from_tick <= to_tick`).
  - `{"type": "accusation", "against": "<player_id>", "confidence": <0.0-1.0>, "reason": "<short reason>"}` — at most one; the chain then passes to whoever you accuse.
  - `{"type": "corroboration", "supports": "<player_id>", "on_tick": <int>, "reason": "<short reason>"}`.
- `free_text` (REQUIRED): one or two sentences stating your conclusion in your own voice.

Exact shape to follow — copy this STRUCTURE (all eight keys, ending with
`free_text`), but substitute the real ticks, rooms, and player ids from YOUR
memory above (these values are only an illustration):
```json
{"turn_id": "t", "turn_index": 0, "speaker": "{{ agent_id }}", "turn_kind": "opening", "reply_to": null, "observations": [{"type": "saw_player", "tick": 12, "subject": "p-1", "room": "CAFETERIA", "co_present": ["p-2"]}], "claims": [{"type": "accusation", "against": "p-1", "confidence": 0.6, "reason": "last seen with the victim right before the kill"}], "free_text": "p-1 was the last one I saw with the victim in CAFETERIA, so they are my main suspect."}
```

Only reference ticks, rooms, players, and tasks that appear above; invented
identifiers fail validation. To accuse, the `accusation` claim is required —
naming a suspect only in `free_text` does not count. Output ONLY the JSON object,
starting with `{` and ending with `}` — and make sure that object includes
`free_text`.
